-- Passage rg5-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b06f7ba0-e674-50fb-b857-d68a32c5876e', 'rg5-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Drawing the World$q$, $q$Long ago, there were no satellites or airplanes to show what the land looked like from above. People who made maps, called cartographers, had to collect information in other ways.

Some mapmakers traveled for months. They walked along coasts and rivers, measuring distances with chains and counting their steps. Others relied on sailors and traders. These travelers described the places they had visited, and the mapmakers drew what they heard.

Because of this, early maps were often inaccurate. A river might bend the wrong way, or an island might be twice its true size. Some maps even showed lands that did not exist at all.

Over many years, maps became more precise. New tools helped people measure angles and distances exactly. Mapmakers compared their work and fixed old errors.

Maps also had to be durable. A paper map used on a ship could get wet, torn, or faded by the sun. For that reason, some sailors kept their maps rolled in leather cases. A strong map could last for many voyages.$q$, 'draft')
